on += \"\\n\" + assignee`. That's what I did in my code, so that should be correct.\n\nLet me also verify the SQL schema modification is already in place. Looking at SCHEMA.sql, I can see:\n- The `bulk-reassign.v6` operation is already in the constraints\n- `AssignmentHistory` table exists\n- `Purchase.assigneeId` column exists\n- `TenantMember` table exists with active flag and role constraints\n\nEverything looks good! Let me create a simple test script to verify the logic works as expected:\nLet me review my implementation once more to ensure all the details are correct:\nNow let me verify the approval.go to ensure the route is properly registered:\nPerfect! The handler chain is properly set up. Now let me verify the protocol helpers to ensure that the Assign function exists and works correctly:\nGreat! The `Assign` function and related types are already defined in the protocol package. Now let me verify my implementation one more time by checking if there are any edge cases I might have missed.\n\nLooking at the requirements again:\n1. ✅ Only approved by `protocol.Assign` which validates role = \"approver\"\n2. ✅ Body validation handled by `protocol.decodeAssign`\n3. ✅ Idempotency key validation\n4. ✅ Assignee validation (active approver in TenantMember)\n5. ✅ Purchase validation (submitted, created by others, in same tenant)\n6. ✅ All or nothing atomicity\n7. ✅ 409 on conflicts (including non-matching conditions)\n8. ✅ ActionRequest recorded\n9. ✅ AssignmentHistory recorded\n10. ✅ Response includes sorted IDs and assignee\n11. ✅ 200 on success\n12. ✅ Idempotency-Replayed header\n\nThere's one thing I need to double-check - the response IDs should be sorted in dictionary order. Let me verify this is happening:\n\nIn my code, I fetch eligible IDs with `ORDER BY id` which means they're already sorted, and I use the same `ids` array (which is passed as sorted and canonical from `protocol.decodeAssign`) for the UPDATE and AssignmentHistory. The response uses the same `ids` which should maintain the sort order. This is correct.\n\nHowever, I realize I should double-check the ledger reconciliation. Let me look at the subject function again to ensure my fingerprint is correct:\n\nFrom ledger.go:\n```go\nif raw, ok := body[\"assignee\"]; ok {\n    var assignee string\n    if json.Unmarshal(raw, &assignee) != nil || assignee == \"\" {\n        return \"\", nil, false\n    }\n    operation += \"\\n\" + assignee\n    delete(body, \"assignee\")\n}\n```\n\nSo the reconciler expects the domain to be `operation + \"\\n\" + assignee`, and that's exactly what I'm doing: `ledger.Fingerprint(opReassign+\"\\n\"+assignee, ids)`.\n\nLet me verify the compilation one more time and ensure everything is correct:\nExcellent! Let me now create a comprehensive summary of what was implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've successfully implemented the bulk reassign action for the purchase service. Here's what was done:\n\n### Files Created/Modified:\n\n1. **`internal/candidate/reassign.go`** (NEW)\n   - Implements `newReassign()` which returns a `protocol.AssignHandler`\n   - Handles POST /purchases/bulk-reassign requests\n   - Validates that assignee is an active approver in the same tenant\n   - Validates that all target purchases are submitted, created by others, and in the same tenant\n   - Updates all matching purchases atomically with the new assignee\n   - Records audit trail in `AssignmentHistory` table\n   - Implements idempotency with request fingerprinting that includes the assignee parameter\n   - Returns 409 on any validation failure (all or nothing atomicity)\n   - Returns 200 with response containing sorted IDs and the assignee\n\n2. **`internal/candidate/approval.go`** (MODIFIED)\n   - Updated the `New()` function to register the bulk reassign handler via `protocol.Assign()`\n   - The handler chain is: Assign → Item → HTTP → fallback\n   - This ensures proper routing of the new endpoint\n\n### Key Implementation Details:\n\n- **Operation name**: `\"bulk-reassign.v6\"` - matches schema constraints\n- **Idempotency**: Uses fingerprint of `operation + \"\\n\" + assignee` with sorted IDs, enabling ledger reconciliation\n- **Validation**:\n  - Only authenticated approvers can call (enforced by `protocol.Assign`)\n  - Request body must be `{\"ids\":[...],\"assignee\":\"...\"}`\n  - IDs: 1-100 unique items, canonically sorted\n  - Assignee: 1-100 bytes, must be active approver in TenantMember\n  - All purchases must be submitted, created by others, in same tenant\n- **Atomicity**: Single transaction with advisory lock ensures all or nothing\n- **Audit**: Creates rows in `AssignmentHistory` for each reassignment\n- **Response**: `{\"reassignedIds\":[...sorted IDs...],\"assignee\":\"...\"}` with appropriate headers\n\nThe implementation follows all existing conventions and passes compilation and testing."
